# Prohibition of Self-Invocation Across Transactional Proxy Boundaries

## 1. Context & Architectural Intent

Spring's `@Transactional` (and other AOP-driven annotations: `@Cacheable`, `@Async`,
`@PreAuthorize`, `@Validated`) are implemented via dynamic proxies. When external code calls a
proxied method, the proxy intercepts the call, opens the transaction, invokes the underlying
method, and commits or rolls back on return. But when a method on a Spring-managed bean calls
**another method on the same instance via `this.someMethod()`**, the proxy is bypassed entirely
— the inner method's `@Transactional` annotation is silently inert.

AI-generated code routinely contains this trap: a public service method calls a private (or
package-private) `@Transactional` helper on the same class, expecting a transaction. The
transaction never opens. Bugs surface in production as "the rollback didn't fire" or "the
isolation level wasn't applied" — diagnostics so disconnected from the cause that they often
go unresolved for quarters.

This rule mandates the two safe patterns (entry-method `@Transactional`, OR extract-to-bean)
and forbids the self-invocation shape entirely.

## 2. Enforced Standards (AI Ingestion Core)

### 2.1. No `@Transactional` on a Method Called Only via `this.method()` from Sibling Methods

* **Rule**: A method annotated with any AOP-driven annotation (`@Transactional`, `@Cacheable`,
  `@Async`, `@PreAuthorize`, `@Validated`) MUST be callable from outside the bean. The proxy
  is the only legal call site for such methods. Sibling-method self-invocation via `this` is
  forbidden — either the entry method MUST carry the annotation, or the annotated method MUST
  be extracted to a separate bean.
* **Rationale**: The proxy is the AOP interceptor. Self-invocation bypasses the proxy and
  drops the annotation's effect on the floor. Spring does not warn about this at startup or
  runtime; the only signal is wrong behavior in production.
* **Implementation Requirement**:

  ```java
  // ❌ ANTI-PATTERN — inner @Transactional silently ignored
  @Service
  @Transactional(readOnly = true)
  @RequiredArgsConstructor
  public class OrderService {
      private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
      private final NotificationGateway notifications;

      public void process(OrderRequest req) {
          this.save(req);              // self-invocation: proxy is bypassed,
          notifications.notify(req);   // @Transactional below is DEAD
      }

      @Transactional(timeout = 5)
      public void save(OrderRequest req) {
          orderRepository.save(new Order(req));
      }
  }

  // ✅ CORRECT (option A): annotate the entry method instead
  @Service
  @Transactional(readOnly = true)
  @RequiredArgsConstructor
  public class OrderService {
      private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
      private final NotificationGateway notifications;

      @Transactional(timeout = 5)
      public void process(OrderRequest req) {
          orderRepository.save(new Order(req));
          notifications.notify(req);
      }
  }

  // ✅ CORRECT (option B): extract the @Transactional method to a separate bean
  @Service
  @RequiredArgsConstructor
  public class OrderPersistenceService {
      private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

      @Transactional(timeout = 5)
      public void save(OrderRequest req) {
          orderRepository.save(new Order(req));
      }
  }

  @Service
  @Transactional(readOnly = true)
  @RequiredArgsConstructor
  public class OrderService {
      private final OrderPersistenceService persistence;     // proxy-routed call
      private final NotificationGateway notifications;

      public void process(OrderRequest req) {
          persistence.save(req);          // proxy-mediated — @Transactional fires
          notifications.notify(req);
      }
  }
  ```

### 2.2. The Same Trap Applies to `@Cacheable`, `@Async`, `@PreAuthorize`, `@Validated`

* **Rule**: Every AOP-driven annotation (not just `@Transactional`) carries the
  self-invocation trap. The two safe patterns are identical: annotate the entry method, or
  extract to a separate bean.
* **Rationale**: All Spring AOP is proxy-based by default. `@Cacheable` self-invocation means
  the cache is never consulted; `@Async` self-invocation means the method runs synchronously;
  `@PreAuthorize` self-invocation means the security check is skipped. The latter is a
  security defect.

### 2.3. Internal Helpers SHOULD NOT Carry AOP Annotations

* **Rule**: A `private` or package-private helper method that exists only to be called by
  sibling methods on the same class SHOULD NOT carry AOP-driven annotations. The intent of
  such helpers is "internal implementation detail"; AOP-driven annotations imply "external
  contract."
* **Rationale**: Placing `@Transactional` on a private helper is a category error — it expects
  proxy interception that cannot happen. The only legal way to AOP-annotate an internal
  helper is to make it public and have external code call it through the proxy — which by
  definition means it is not an internal helper.

### 2.4. The `AopContext.currentProxy()` Workaround Is Forbidden

* **Rule**: The `((OrderService) AopContext.currentProxy()).save(req)` workaround MUST NOT be
  used. The two safe patterns (annotate the entry method, or extract to a bean) are always
  preferable.
* **Rationale**: `AopContext.currentProxy()` requires `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy = true)`
  globally, leaks Spring's AOP details into business code, and creates a circular dependency
  between the bean and its own proxy that obscures the code's intent.

## 3. AI Directives

When generating, modifying, or refactoring Java code under `**/service/**/*.java` or
`**/usecase/**/*.java`:

1. **Before placing `@Transactional` (or any AOP-driven annotation) on a method, verify the
   method is callable from outside the bean.** If only sibling methods call it, refactor: move
   the annotation to the entry method, OR extract the annotated method to a separate bean.
2. **When the user shows code where a public method calls `this.someMethod()` and `someMethod`
   is `@Transactional`, flag the self-invocation trap** and propose one of the two safe
   patterns in the response.
3. **Never propose `AopContext.currentProxy()` as a fix.** Always propose entry-method
   annotation or bean extraction.
4. **For any `@Cacheable`, `@Async`, `@PreAuthorize`, `@Validated` placement, apply the same
   self-invocation check.** The trap is identical across all AOP-driven annotations.
